Article 2 The term "coastal engineering construction projects" as used in these Regulations refers to new construction, renovation, and expansion projects that are located on or connected to the coast, with the main body of the project on the landward side of the coastline, and that have an impact on the marine environment. They specifically include:
(1) port, dock, channel, and coastal airport projects;
(2) shipyard and repair dock projects;
(3) coastal thermal power plants, nuclear power plants, and wind power stations;
(4) coastal material storage facility projects;
(5) coastal industrial projects such as mining, chemical, light industry, and metallurgy;
(6) projects for the treatment, disposal, and discharge of pollutants such as solid waste and sewage into the sea;
(7) large coastal aquaculture farms;
(8) coastal protection works, sand and stone yards, and water conservancy facilities at estuarine outlets;
(9) coastal petroleum exploration and development projects;
(10) other coastal engineering projects as prescribed by the competent department of environmental protection under the State Council jointly with the State oceanic administrative department.

Article 8 In addition to the preparation in accordance with the relevant provisions, the contents of the environmental impact report of a coastal engineering construction project shall also include:
(1) the environmental conditions of the site and its adjacent sea areas;
(2) the possible impacts on the marine environment during and after construction;
(3) the marine environmental protection measures and the conclusions on the technical and economic feasibility demonstration;
(4) the conclusions of the marine environmental impact assessment of the construction project.
The environmental impact report form of a coastal engineering construction project shall be filled in by reference to the preceding paragraph.
